---
title: "Image annotation"
description: "What a local raster image gets for free: a reserved box and a loading policy."
---

Every local raster image on this page is annotated at *render* time with its intrinsic
size, so the reader's layout is reserved before a single byte of the image arrives. The
build copies your file across unchanged; it derives nothing and re-encodes nothing.

## A captioned figure

The first image in a document is the one most likely to be the largest contentful paint,
so it is deliberately **not** lazy: it is fetched eagerly at high priority. Lazy-loading an
above-the-fold image delays the very metric the annotation exists to improve.

![A model fit, wide enough to be worth measuring.](fit-a.png){#fig-opt-wide}

## An inline image

A bare image in a paragraph is not a figure, but it is still a raster file the reader has
to download, so it gets the same treatment minus the numbering: ![a small
fit](fit-small.png) sits in this sentence.

## A second raster, below the fold

Every image after the first gets `loading="lazy"`, whatever its size. `fit-small.png` is
320 pixels wide and is annotated with exactly that, read from the file itself rather than
guessed from the markup.

![The same fit, smaller.](fit-small.png){#fig-opt-narrow}

## What is left alone

An SVG has no intrinsic pixel size to state, and a `data:` URI already carries its own
pixels, so neither is annotated.
